   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-14-apollo-12-and-surveyor-3/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: Put on your red/blue glasses and gaze across the western Ocean of Storms on the surface of the Moon. The 3D anaglyph shows Apollo 12 astronaut Pete Conrad visiting the Surveyor 3 spacecraft in November of 1969.",
   "kredi": "Apollo 12, Alan Bean - Stereo Image Copyright: Kevin Frank",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  },
  {
   "tarih": "2026-11-13",
   "baslik": "NGC 206 and the Star Clouds of Andromeda",
   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-13-ngc-206-and-the-star-clouds-of-andromeda/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: The large stellar association cataloged as NGC 206 is nestled within the dusty arms of the neighboring Andromeda galaxy along with the galaxy's pinkish star-forming regions. Also known as M31, the spiral galaxy is a mere 2.5 million light-years away.",
   "kredi": "David Cruz",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  },
  {
   "tarih": "2026-11-12",
   "baslik": "Moon, Venus and Spica Meet",
   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-12-moon-venus-and-spica-meet/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: Not all those who wander are lost. A night sky example is that planets wander, and in fact the very word \"planet\" comes from \"wandering star\" in Greek. As planets shift relative to fixed stars, they may appear from our perspective to get closer to other astronomical objects.",
   "kredi": "Włodzimierz Bubak",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  },
  {
   "tarih": "2026-11-11",
   "baslik": "NGC 4449: Small Galaxy, Big Impact",
   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-11-ngc-4449-small-galaxy-big-impact/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: Don’t judge a galaxy by its size. Our home galaxy, the Milky Way, is about 100,000 light years across. This huge expanse of space contains hundreds of billions of stars! The smallest galaxies, called dwarf galaxies, are about 1/10th the size of the Milky Way and only contain a few billion stars.",
   "kredi": "NASA, ESA, A. Aloisi (STScI/ESA), and the Hubble Heritage (STScI/AURA)-ESA/Hubble Collaboration",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  },
  {
   "tarih": "2026-11-10",
   "baslik": "Saturn Behind the Moon",
   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-10-saturn-behind-the-moon/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: Sometimes Saturn disappears. It doesn't really go away, of course -- it just disappears from our view when Earth's Moon moves in front.",
   "kredi": "Roberto Ortu",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
